m-boot bootable-media menus

PURPOSE
    The ESP-resident m-loader provides "Bootable media" and four categories:
    Recovery and backup, Live systems and installers, Hardware diagnostics,
    and Network boot. ISO files live on ext4 MBOOTDATA, not on the ESP.
    The menu is generated from files that actually exist at boot time.

DISK PATHS
    ext4 MBOOTDATA: /m/boot/iso/*.iso
                   /m/boot/efi/memtest/*   (optional unpacked payloads)
                   /m/boot/efi/netboot/*   (optional iPXE payloads)
    FAT32 MBOOTESP: /m/boot/loader/
                   /m/boot/loader/menu/boot-media*
                   /m/boot/loader/menu/iso-boot/ (preserved legacy recipes)

CATEGORIES AND TEST ACTIONS
    Recovery and backup:
      x86 BIOS/UEFI: Clonezilla and GParted historical Linux-live recipes,
      Grml AMD64 (ISO GRUB prefix and findiso handoff), Finnix,
      SystemRescue, Super Grub2 classic, HBCD_PE.
      Rescuezilla also exposes a dedicated casper kernel/initrd attempt.
      ARM64 UEFI: only matching Grml ARM64 images, with ISO-specific
      GRUB prefix and findiso parameter propagation.

    Live systems and installers:
      Native-architecture Ubuntu desktop ISO (AMD64 on x86, ARM64 on ARM).
      Proxmox VE native architecture; AMD64 also lists Proxmox Backup Server,
      TrueNAS SCALE and OPNsense DVD ISO. OPNsense .iso.bz2 is a compressed
      archive, not an additional bootable ISO.

    Hardware diagnostics:
      Memtest86+ 9.00 from unpacked native binaries under
      /m/boot/efi/memtest/ (x86_64/i586 GRUB linux/linux16,
      ARM64 UEFI chainloading). The ISO is inspection-only.

    Network boot:
      netboot.xyz multiarch ISO and optional architecture-matched iPXE
      native EFI or BIOS .lkrn files.

    Each experimental ISO submenu provides:
      - Try ISO GRUB configuration: use ISO /boot/grub/loopback.cfg where
        available, else try its /boot/grub/grub.cfg (may assume optical media).
      - Try embedded EFI application: on matching UEFI only, probe
        the real ISO9660 case-sensitive variants of EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI,
        EFI/BOOT/BOOTx64.EFI, EFI/BOOT/bootx64.efi,
        EFI/boot/bootx64.efi and
        efi/boot/bootx64.efi (or corresponding BOOTAA64/bootaa64).
        A matching file is not proof it can run from GRUB loopback.
      - Inspect ISO files and boot paths: list GRUB/EFI directories in GRUB.
    Missing files produce an explicit diagnostic instead of a false PASS.

    For ISO images exposing only /boot/grub/grub.cfg rather than
    loopback.cfg (for example the scanned TrueNAS and Proxmox media),
    the experimental GRUB configuration action now scopes GRUB prefix
    to the mounted ISO while reading that configuration, then restores
    the ESP prefix. Images with loopback.cfg (including Ubuntu) retain
    their previous boot path. Native ISO GRUB menus may still need a
    real block/optical device after kernel handoff.


BOOT COMPATIBILITY AND LIMITATIONS
    Actual operator ISO inventory on 2026-11-01: 18 .iso files
    successfully inspected, with no catalog/path inspection errors.
    Memtest86+ 9.00 native x86_64/i586 files are Linux boot protocol
    bzImages, and ARM64/LoongArch variants are EFI PE32+ applications.
    Grml full 2026.09 both AMD64 and ARM64 contain loopback.cfg.
    Proxmox VE 9.2-1 AMD64/ARM64 and PBS 4.2-1 expose grub.cfg and
    EFI fallbacks, with case-sensitive lower-case efi/boot paths.
    TrueNAS SCALE 25.10.7 exposes grub.cfg, /vmlinuz and /initrd.img;
    its installer also searches for its installation medium.
    HBCD PE x64 exposes /bootmgr, /boot/boot.sdi and
    /sources/boot.wim, requiring a Windows PE loader or suitable
    virtual optical drive; it does not provide a generic Linux
    kernel/initrd entry. OPNsense 26.7 DVD is a FreeBSD-style ISO
    with BIOS El Torito records in the inspected output, and lacks
    a normal GRUB grub.cfg. These are inventory properties,
    not confirmed installer compatibility.

    A GRUB loopback mount reads an ISO but does NOT expose it as a physical
    virtual CD device to Linux, Windows PE or BSD early userspace.
    A boot menu can execute while the selected operating system still fails
    to mount the original ISO. All fallback GRUB/EFI attempts are experimental
    until the exact ISO version reaches its usable live system or installer.
    Some products, including Proxmox installers, TrueNAS, OPNsense, HBCD_PE,
    and netboot.xyz ISO BIOS path, can require a dedicated media emulation
    bridge/boot recipe; their menu presence is not proof of compatibility.

    User's first post-update test reports Memtest86+, Grml and HBCD PE
    failing, while Clonezilla, GParted, Finnix, SystemRescue,
    Super Grub2, Rescuezilla, and netboot.xyz/iPXE work; the installer
    category except Ubuntu does not work. These are operator-reported
    outcomes, not detailed per-image kernel/root diagnostics. Changes
    after that report provide native Memtest direct entries and a Grml
    ISO-specific GRUB prefix. Neither improvement has been physically
    validated. HBCD PE and most installers retain experimental attempts;
    robust virtual-CD/early-userspace compatibility remains open.

    The legacy ISO recipes from aguslr/multibootusb remain present on ESP.
    Clonezilla and GParted use their retained historical recipes. Ubuntu
    AMD64 26.04.1 has operator-reported successful transition beyond the
    former BusyBox ash failure into the live installer, on the physical
    ext4 stick in x86 legacy BIOS QEMU (2026-11-01). This was very slow
    emulation and is not a completed install or all-platform acceptance.
    Tested menu/ISO versions and architectures must be reported separately.

UPDATE MENUS WITHOUT REFLASHING
    Changes to loader/menu/boot-media* in Git DO NOT appear on a previously
    flashed USB automatically. The current install.sh intentionally refuses
    overwriting an existing loader/EFI tree. No rebuild or whole-disk dd is
    necessary for GRUB script-only edits because m-loader reads these scripts
    from the existing FAT32 ESP dynamically.

    Identify the exact MBOOTESP partition by device model/serial and label;
    safely stop QEMU and unmount ext4 first. Mount ONLY that FAT32 partition
    at a temporary directory (not / or the ext4 data partition), verify
    the installed m/boot/loader/menu exists, and back it up on the host.
    Copy the repository's loader/menu/boot-media* files to the existing
    ESP m/boot/loader/menu/ directory, verify the copies by cmp, sync,
    then unmount the ESP. Preserve EFI/BOOT, loader/init, GRUB module
    directories, themes, and all ext4 ISO data unchanged.

    If a file copy or comparison fails, restore the backed-up menu files
    and do not boot from a potentially partial update. Never rerun
    usb-install.sh to update menus: that command ERASES the entire disk.

VALIDATION
    GitHub's validate-boot-media workflow uses the versioned GRUB 2.12
    grub-script-check against all current category/helper files and checks
    references. Parser PASS is necessary but not firmware boot proof.
    Validate x86 BIOS, x86 UEFI and ARM64 UEFI individually; QEMU on ARM
    virt needs an explicit virtual keyboard controller for menu navigation.
    Validate the loader-to-ISO transition separately from full live root.
    Mount/unmount USB safely and use QEMU snapshot-only for physical tests.

